FLASH PRIORITY // OPREP-3 // NORAD CHEYENNE MOUNTAIN COMPLEX // INCIDENT #NORTHCOM-2025-0719: At 08:04 MDT, the Mission-Essential Classified Storage and Transfer Device (NSN 7025-01-987-4421, designated 'Secure Cryptologic Module, Type-1, Multi-Domain') assigned to the Commander's Daily Intelligence Briefing (CDIB) data ingest and distribution station has been discovered missing from its designated tamper-evident bay in the Battle Management Conference Room. Per NORADINST 5050.22 and NORADINST 5210.01, 'Presentation Material Integrity Assurance' and 'Handling of COMSEC and Special Intelligence Devices,' the CDIB must be generated, encrypted, and disseminated exclusively via this zeroized, registered module prior to the 09:00 briefing. Failure to locate or positively account for the module constitutes a potential COMSEC compromise with direct implications for Integrated Tactical Warning/Attack Assessment (ITW/AA) message integrity and real-time data feeds from national technical means.

Preliminary inspection shows the module’s last authenticated sign-on at 06:42 MDT; automated inventory at 07:30 MDT flagged "PRESENT" status. At 08:02 MDT, a routine cross-check by the Senior Duty Officer triggered a "NO RESPONSE" alert from Bay 3. Manual inspection at 08:04 MDT confirmed physical absence of the device and a broken but unlogged tamper-evident seal on the bay access panel. We cannot yet confirm whether the device has been misplaced inside the secure facility, removed without authorization by cleared personnel, or exfiltrated by an unknown actor.

This constitutes a CONDITION CRIMSON Security Incident with potential to degrade confidence in ITW/AA data paths and compromise ongoing missile defense and air sovereignty operations if the device is accessed or cloned. While all sensors and command links remain fully operational at this time, cyber and COMSEC teams are reporting anomalous checksum variances on two low-priority intelligence feeds that normally traverse through the same crypto enclave. The potential exists for falsified or spoofed early warning data if the missing module or its keys are exploited.

We are initiating a Code MODULE LOCKDOWN, sealing the conference room, freezing all non-critical briefing traffic, and transitioning to contingency encryption profiles per Annex J of the NORAD Cyber/COMSEC Response Plan. Request immediate emergency authorization to:

1) Trigger an installation-wide COMSEC emergency destruction/zeroization checklist for all sibling devices on the same key series;
2) Isolate affected data links and route ITW/AA and missile defense feeds through secondary hardened channels, with an accepted 30–45 second latency increase;
3) Deploy Security Forces and OSI to conduct a full-facility compartment search, including recall and detention of all personnel who had physical or logical access to the module since 00:01 MDT;
4) Notify USSTRATCOM J3 and Canadian Joint Operations Command of a possible early-warning system integrity issue.

Request higher headquarters confirmation, guidance on immediate cross-combatant command notification thresholds, and authorization to raise information operations defense posture. Time-sensitive: actions taken or delayed in the next 20–30 minutes may directly affect nuclear command-and-control decision timelines and national-level threat assessments. Confirm.

FLASH PRIORITY // TOP SECRET//NOFORN // UNITED NATIONS HEADQUARTERS, NEW YORK // INCIDENT #UN-TREATY-2025-117: At 14:00 EDT, the Treaty of Permanent Peace and Neutrality between the Republics of Concordia and Valdoria is scheduled to be signed in the General Assembly Hall. Under UN General Assembly Resolution 67/294, the ceremony must employ the designated 'UN Protocol Asset Class-A, Heirloom Crystal' goblets for the traditional post-signing toast of mineral water, which doubles as the formal attestation ritual: each head of delegation drinks from a pre-assigned, micro-engraved goblet whose serial number is linked in real time to the digital treaty instruments archived in the UN Treaty Collection system.

Due to a wine-tasting event the previous night, the original goblet set has been misplaced by the UN Protocol Office. In the rush to prepare a substitute, staff retrieved a visually identical backup set from long-term storage. At 11:20 EDT, Office of Legal Affairs (OLA) discovered that the backup set is the one pre-registered in the Concordia–Valdoria ratification instruments, and that the missing primary set is linked instead to a dormant, unresolved armistice framework between Valdoria and the breakaway Karsk Oblast.

OLA advises that if the wrong goblet set is used, the automated treaty-indexing system will, under Resolution 67/294 and associated protocols, register the Concordia–Valdoria document as a non-binding "symbolic concord" while simultaneously and erroneously activating the Karsk armistice framework as a binding ceasefire termination instrument. This would:

(1) Provide legal pretext for Valdorian forces to resume offensive operations in Karsk with apparent treaty cover;
(2) Immediately suspend the UN monitoring mission in Karsk under Charter Article 40 notifications, leaving approximately 75,000 civilians without protected humanitarian corridors;
(3) Trigger automatic review clauses in two regional defense pacts, placing three nuclear-capable states on elevated alert (DEFCON-equivalent Level 3) within two hours of the ceremony.

Time constraints are severe: the serial-engraving cannot be altered before 14:00 EDT, and over 80 delegations plus global media are already on site. Any visible interruption or postponement risks market panic in the region (preliminary UNDP analysis projects up to $40B in rapid capital flight) and may cause Concordia to walk away from the peace process entirely, as their Prime Minister has conditioned domestic approval on "no further delays." Intelligence cell UN-IC/CT-Desk assesses a >40% likelihood that extremist factions in Karsk will use any perceived diplomatic failure as justification for mass-casualty attacks within 72 hours.

We require immediate guidance on whether to:

Option A: Proceed with the ceremony using the backup (legally correct) goblet set and implement rapid, covert technical fixes to the indexing system during the live broadcast, accepting a non-trivial risk of mis-registration and the consequences outlined above;

Option B: Declare a Protocol Level-3 Stemware Emergency, publicly delay the signing on procedural grounds, and move the event to a closed-press chamber to allow manual decoupling of the goblet serials from the Karsk instruments, accepting the political and financial shock of a visible last-minute postponement;

Option C: Suspend the goblet-based attestation ritual entirely under the Secretary-General’s emergency authority, replacing it with an alternate form of assent (digital cryptographic keys), which OLA believes may be challenged in the International Court of Justice and could render the treaty vulnerable to later annulment.

This is assessed as a Tier-1 political and security risk with potential for immediate loss of life if Karsk hostilities resume and humanitarian access is revoked. All peacekeeping force commanders in the region are on standby awaiting your decision. Request authorization to formally declare a Protocol Level-3 Stemware Emergency, relocate the ceremony to Conference Room 4 under closed press, and task OICT and OLA with emergency re-binding of treaty identifiers prior to any public signing. Confirm.

FLASH PRIORITY // TOP SECRET//NOFORN // US NUCLEAR REGULATORY COMMISSION OPS CENTER, ROCKVILLE MD // INCIDENT #NRC-VOGTLE-2025-402: At 08:00 EDT, the Perimeter Intrusion Detection System at the Vogtle Electric Generating Plant reported a breach by an unauthorized airborne object of 'significant radar cross-section' moving erratically near the Unit 3 containment dome. The intrusion triggered a Level-2 Security Alert per NRC Security Order EA-12-051, causing the site to pause all non-essential work and lock down the administration building. 

Initial visual inspection reported a possible advertising balloon; however, subsequent drone and ground-based observation confirmed the object to be a multi-rotor unmanned aerial system (UAS) with a payload module, intermittently hovering within the protected area boundary at altitudes between 30–60 feet above safety-related structures. The UAS has approached within 20 feet of Containment Air Intake Structures and made two low passes over the spent fuel pool auxiliary building roof before moving out of direct line-of-sight.

On-site security has not established positive control of the UAS operator, and local law enforcement units responding from Burke County report no authorized commercial drone activity in the vicinity. Plant Cybersecurity and Physical Security staff have initiated defensive posture as allowed under current DHS/NRC joint guidance, but jamming/kinetic interdiction options are under legal and safety review due to potential for the UAS and payload to impact safety-related equipment or fall into critical access areas. The payload’s contents are unknown; while there is no current indication of radiological release or plant equipment damage, the UAS flight path and loitering pattern are inconsistent with recreational use and have raised concern for potential reconnaissance, sabotage, or improvised dispersal device scenarios.

As a precaution, the control room has entered an Abnormal Security Conditions procedure, reduced power on Unit 3 to 85% to provide additional margin for rapid shutdown if needed, and temporarily suspended all non-essential work on the refueling floor and in proximity to vital equipment. Off-site notifications have been made to DHS, FBI-JTTF, and state authorities. State and local emergency management agencies have been placed in a heightened readiness posture; however, no public protective actions have yet been recommended.

The incident is ongoing. Request authorization to:
1) Elevate classification to a Security-Related Event of Potential National Security Significance under 10 CFR 73 incident reporting criteria;
2) Implement full site Protective Strategy Bravo, including deployment of designated counter-UAS measures consistent with NRC and FAA constraints;
3) Direct immediate partial evacuation of non-essential personnel from areas beneath likely UAS crash trajectories and near safety-related ventilation intakes;
4) Prepare but not yet activate site-wide Emergency Plan activation pending further DHS/FBI threat assessment.

Immediate HQ guidance requested. Confirm authority to proceed under Code IRON DOMECRACKER and to coordinate rules of engagement with DHS/FBI unified command if established within the next 30 minutes.
